; ltc_gen.loom
; SMPTE linear timecode (LTC) generator on OUT0: 80-bit frames, biphase mark
; code, at 24, 25, 30 or 29.97 drop-frame frames a second, counting from a
; time the host sets.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). Nothing in Loom was built for
; timecode or for biphase mark code. The program uses two pieces of hardware
; built for other jobs: the deadline-latched pin write (`SETP pin, v, D`,
; SEMANTICS 6.10, D-016), built so that WS2812 pulses land on the exact
; clock, puts every edge of the code on the exact clock edge of its half-bit
; tick; and the data memory (`LD`/`ST`, SEMANTICS 6.11, D-027), built for
; device emulation (a 24C02), holds the time and the frame's four data
; words. What the firmware does that no hardware does: the biphase mark code
; itself, the frame (BCD fields, the drop-frame flag, the sync word), the
; polarity correction bit, and the clock: it counts frames, seconds, minutes
; and hours, with the drop-frame rule at 29.97.
;
; The bit engine does not help. Its Manchester encoder (SEMANTICS 6.9.1) is
; IEEE 802.3 Manchester, where the level in each half of a bit is fixed by
; the bit; biphase mark fixes only where the edges are. Biphase mark is NRZI
; (the engine's USB encoder, a 0 toggles) of the half-bit stream 0, ~b for
; each bit b, so the engine could send it from a shift register holding the
; frame doubled and inverted in alternate bits, but `SHO` writes the pin in
; its own slot, on the 4-clock slot grid, not on the tick, and the engine
; has no deadline-latched form: the edges would be 0 to 3 clocks late and
; the program would spend as many words building the doubled words as it
; saves. The firmware keeps the line level in r6 and toggles it.
;
; Pin: TX = OUT0, push-pull. Low until RUN; the code is polarity-free, so
; the first edge is a rise.
;
; Sources: the public descriptions of SMPTE ST 12-1 (Wikipedia, "Linear
; timecode" and "SMPTE timecode", read 2026-11-07; secondary, ST 12-1 itself
; was not read), cited by item in tools/protomodels/ltc.py, which is written
; from them: the bit assignment, the sync word 0011 1111 1111 1101 in bits
; 64..79, biphase mark code, the polarity bit (27, or 59 at 25 fps) that
; makes the number of 0s in a frame even, the drop-frame rule.
;
; Host words (FIFO space, thread 0), in this order after RUN:
;   1. TICK_INT of the half-bit, in clocks: 1 / (160 x frame rate). At
;      50 MHz: 13,020 (24 fps), 12,500 (25), 10,416 (30), 10,427 (29.97);
;      184 or more (.tick).
;   2. TICK_FRAC of the half-bit (213, 0, 171, 21 at 50 MHz).
;   3. The frames a second in BCD: 0x24, 0x25 or 0x30 (29.97 too).
;   4. 0x400 for drop-frame counting (the flag's place in word 0, bit 10),
;      else 0.
;   5..8. The time of the first frame in BCD, one field a word: frames,
;      seconds, minutes, hours. The host gives a valid time (not a dropped
;      label at 29.97).
; Nothing is pushed to OUTQ.
;
; The stream. The first thing sent is a sync word, then the first frame's
; data bits 0..63, its sync word 64..79, the next frame, and so on: a
; reader locks on the first sync word and reads the first frame whole.
; Bit 0 of a frame starts exactly 160 ticks after bit 0 of the one before.
; The program is a free-running clock: its frame is the board crystal's,
; and the host aligns it to a video reference by timing word 8. The first
; edge lands 2 ticks after word 8 is popped.
;
; Coding. Each edge is `SETP TX, v, D` and the WAITD 1 that lands it, on
; the exact clock edge of its tick (SEMANTICS 6.10 rule 1): an edge at the
; start of every bit and one in its middle for a 1 (the middle WAITD of a
; 0 lands nothing). With a whole number of clocks a tick every edge is on
; the half-bit grid exactly; with TICK_FRAC the ticks dither by one clock
; (SEMANTICS 4), 20 ns at 50 MHz against a 208 us half-bit.
;
; Counting. Each data word is the field's BCD byte with the tens moved to
; bits 8 and up, (x & 15) | (x >> 4) << 8, the frame layout's own. The next
; frame is built while the sync word goes out, one step in each bit's first
; half, so that no half-bit holds more than one step and no loop runs
; between two deadlines: bits 64..67 each add the carry to one field
; (frames, seconds, minutes, hours) and rebuild its word, bit 68 applies
; the drop-frame rule (a carry into a minute whose units digit is not 0
; skips frames 0 and 1), bit 69 adds the drop-frame flag and the polarity
; bit: the sync word holds 13 ones, so the bit is set when the data's ones
; are even. The colour-frame flag, the user bits and BGF0..2 are sent as 0.
;
; Timing. The longest half-bit is bit 69's first, the polarity step: 44
; slots (176 clocks) from the WAITD that lands the bit's edge to the WAITD
; that lands its middle, with the step's loads and stores at two slots
; each. The schedule is proved (20 pairs, no `.bounded`) for a tick of 184
; clocks or more, 8 clocks of slack: any board clock of 0.9 MHz or more at
; 30 fps (0.71 MHz at 24). The tests run 256 clocks a half-bit.
;
; Registers: r0 the word being sent, r1 its bits left, r2 a field record,
; r3 the drop-frame flag as bit 10 (0x400 or 0), r4 the carry, r5 the words
; left, then scratch, r6 the line level, r7 scratch.
;
; Size: 128 words, 116 of code and 12 of data (the four field records),
; exactly a quarter of the 512-word memory. With ltc_rx.loom (107 words)
; on thread 1 the two run together, threads 2 and 3 keep their quarters.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07): SMPTE ST 12-1 was
; not read; every figure is secondary (two encyclopedia articles).
;   24 fps uses the 30 fps bit layout (polarity bit 27); the articles show
;   only the 30 and 25 fps columns.
;   Polarity: an even number of 0s in the frame, sync word included.
;   User bits and the colour-frame and BGF flags are sent as 0.
;   29.97 fps runs at 80 x 30/1.001 bit/s, with the drop-frame rule.
;   One sync word is sent before the first frame, so a reader can take
;   the first frame whole. No tolerance is given by the sources, so the
;   generator is checked for exact edges only.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   TX = OUT0
        .tick   184                     ; the shortest half-bit the schedule is proved for
        .equ    SYNCW  = 0xBFFC         ; bits 64..79, bit 64 in bit 0

        JMP     start                   ; the field records sit low, so LDI reaches them
; The field records: value (BCD), wrap value, data word, for the frames,
; seconds, minutes and hours.
tim:    .word   0, 0, 0                 ; frames (the wrap from host word 3)
        .word   0, 0x60, 0              ; seconds
        .word   0, 0x60, 0              ; minutes
        .word   0, 0x24, 0              ; hours

start:  SETP    TX, 0                   ; low until the first edge
        LDI     r6, 0                   ; and the level says so
        POP     r7                      ; 1. TICK_INT of the half-bit
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r7
        POP     r7                      ; 2. TICK_FRAC
        CSRW    TICK_FRAC, r7
        LDI     r2, tim
        POP     r7                      ; 3. frames a second, BCD
        ST      r7, r2, 1               ; the frames' wrap value
        POP     r3                      ; 4. 0x400 for drop frame (bit 10), else 0
        LDI     r1, 4
tload:  POP     r7                      ; 5..8. frames, seconds, minutes, hours
        ST      r7, r2, 0
        ADDI    r2, 3
        DJNZ    r1, tload
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; the first frame is the host's time
        SETD    1                       ; re-anchor after the unbounded POPs

; The sync word, bits 64..79, and the next frame built in its first halves.
sync0:  MOV16   r0, SYNCW
        LDI     r1, 16
        LDI     r2, tim
sbit:   CALL    edge
        WAITD   1                       ; the edge at the start of the bit
        CMPI    r1, 13
        BC      fstep                   ; bits 64..67: a field each
        CMPI    r1, 12
        BZ      drop                    ; bit 68
        CMPI    r1, 11
        BZ      polar                   ; bit 69
smid:   SHRI    r0, 1
        BNC     sz
        CALL    edge
sz:     WAITD   1
        DJNZ    r1, sbit

; A frame's data bits 0..63, four words of 16, bit 0 first.
frame:  LDI     r2, tim + 2             ; word 0
        LDI     r5, 4
word:   LD      r0, r2, 0
        ADDI    r2, 3
        LDI     r1, 16
dbit:   CALL    edge
        WAITD   1                       ; the edge at the start of the bit
        SHRI    r0, 1                   ; C: a 1
        BNC     dz
        CALL    edge
dz:     WAITD   1                       ; the middle: an edge for a 1
        DJNZ    r1, dbit
        DJNZ    r5, word
        LDI     r4, 1                   ; the next frame is one later
        JMP     sync0

; A field record at r2: add the carry r4, pass a carry on if the field
; wraps, rebuild the word.
fstep:  LD      r5, r2, 0
        TEST    r4, r4
        BZ      fword                   ; no carry: the word only
        INC     r5                      ; add one, in BCD
        MOV     r7, r5
        ANDI    r7, 15
        CMPI    r7, 10
        BNZ     fwrap
        ADDI    r5, 6                   ; the units carry into the tens
fwrap:  LD      r7, r2, 1
        CMP     r5, r7
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; LDI keeps Z: the carry stops here
        BNZ     fst
        LDI     r5, 0                   ; wrapped: the carry goes on
        LDI     r4, 1
fst:    ST      r5, r2, 0
fword:  MOV     r7, r5                  ; (x & 15) | (x >> 4) << 8
        ANDI    r7, 15
        SHRI    r5, 4
        SHLI    r5, 8
        OR      r5, r5, r7
        ST      r5, r2, 2
        ADDI    r2, 3
        JMP     smid

; Drop frame: a carry into a minute that is not a multiple of ten (its
; units digit is not 0) starts the minute at frame 2.
drop:   TEST    r3, r3
        BZ      smid
        LDI     r2, tim
        LD      r5, r2, 0               ; frames and seconds both 0: a new minute
        LD      r7, r2, 3
        OR      r5, r5, r7
        BNZ     smid
        LD      r5, r2, 6
        ANDI    r5, 15
        BZ      smid                    ; minutes 00, 10, .., 50 keep frames 0, 1
        LDI     r5, 2
        ST      r5, r2, 0               ; frame 2, and its word
        ST      r5, r2, 2
        JMP     smid

; The drop-frame flag into word 0, then the polarity bit: set when the ones
; of bits 0..63 are even (the sync word has 13), bit 27 (word 1 bit 11), or
; 59 (word 3 bit 11) at 25 fps.
polar:  LDI     r2, tim
        LD      r5, r2, 2
        OR      r5, r5, r3
        ST      r5, r2, 2
        LD      r7, r2, 5
        XOR     r5, r5, r7
        LD      r7, r2, 8
        XOR     r5, r5, r7
        LD      r7, r2, 11
        XOR     r5, r5, r7
        PAR     r5, r5                  ; C: the data's ones are odd
        BC      smid
        LD      r7, r2, 1
        CMPI    r7, 0x25
        BNZ     pol27
        ADDI    r2, 6                   ; 25 fps: word 3
pol27:  LD      r7, r2, 5
        MOV16   r5, 0x0800
        OR      r7, r7, r5
        ST      r7, r2, 5
        JMP     smid

; edge: stage the next edge (the level r6 toggles) for the next WAITD.
edge:   XORI    r6, 1
        BZ      elo
        SETP    TX, 1, D
        RET
elo:    SETP    TX, 0, D
        RET
